/*! \page mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering Example for spectral rendering

<div align="right">
    [\link mi_neuray_example_axf_to_mdl Previous\endlink]
    [\link mi_neuray_examples Up\endlink]
</div>

The MDL SDK has extended its physically based rendering capabilities with spectral rendering,
allowing materials to be described and evaluated spectrally (per wavelength) instead of only
in RGB color space. This enables more accurate representation of effects such as
colored dispersion, complex absorption in transmissive media, and the correct interaction of
narrow-band light sources with highly saturated materials. With spectral rendering enabled,
MDL materials can drive renderers that operate per wavelength while maintaining the familiar
high-level material descriptions and BSDF/EDF concepts.

A few MDL SDK examples have been extended to demonstrate spectral rendering, such as the 
CPU-based renderer [\link mi_neuray_example_df_native df_native\endlink], and the GPU-based
renderers like the Microsoft DXR-based renderer [\link mi_neuray_example_dxr dxr\endlink] or
the Vulkan-based renderer [\link mi_neuray_example_df_vulkan df_vulkan\endlink]. In this guide,
we describe step by step how to integrate spectral rendering into your rendering application,
using the extended df_native example as a reference, while pointing the few differences to
dxr and df_vulkan examples.

\section mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_new New Topics

- Integrating spectral rendering support into your rendering application

\section mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_descr Detailed Description

\subsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_overview Overview

The original [\link mi_neuray_example_df_native df_native\endlink] example demonstrates how to 
compile MDL materials for the native backend and use the generated BSDF/EDF functions in a 
CPU-based path tracer. The spectral extension builds on this and extends RGB evaluation 
with per-wavelength evaluation while reusing the same MDL materials, BSDF/EDF APIs, and rendering 
algorithm structure.

The integration can be divided into the following steps:

- Configure the number of spectral samples.
- Command line options to control spectral rendering.
- Enable spectral code generation in the MDL backend.
- Propagate the spectral configuration to the shader compilers (GPU backends).
- Switch to spectral shading state types and sample wavelengths per path.
- Use spectral BSDF/EDF data structures.
- Track spectral weights and information to implement spectral MIS.
- Implement RGB to spectral conversion (using the SDK defaults or custom callbacks).
- Apply backend-specific adjustments for DXR and Vulkan.

The following sections go through each step in detail so that you can apply the same changes in
your own renderer.

\subsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_samples Spectral Samples Configuration

For spectral rendering, the MDL SDK introduces a dedicated spectral color representation:

\code
/// A floatN type for spectral sampling.
struct tct_spectral_sample {
    float values[MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES];
};
\endcode

The preprocessor macro \c MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES specifies the number of wavelength samples
carried along each path. By default, this is set to \c 5 in the SDK.

This configuration does not affect the physical correctness or final quality of spectral
rendering in a progressive path tracer, since the solution will always converge to the same
result as more iterations are accumulated. The primary impact is on performance and convergence
behavior: increasing the number of spectral samples can reduce the number of iterations required
to reach a given noise level, but each iteration becomes more expensive to compute.
Consequently, this setting is intended to match the number of spectral samples used by your
rendering application, rather than to change the final rendered quality.

If your renderer requires a different number of spectral samples, you can override the default
at build time by setting the CMake option:

- \c MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES_OVERRIDE

This allows you to align the MDL runtime's spectral layout with your renderer's internal spectral
sampling scheme.

\note libbsdf treats the value at index \c 0 of \c tct_spectral_sample as the <em>main wavelength</em>:
this is the wavelength that BSDF/EDF sampling routines use as the reference for importance sampling.
The remaining entries are evaluated alongside the main wavelength so that all samples share the same
sampled direction. The renderer is expected to fill \c spectral_wavelengths with one main wavelength
followed by additional wavelengths; how these are placed or sampled across the wavelength range
is up to the renderer. See
\ref mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_state below for the per-ray sampling code used by the
df_native example.

\subsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_cmdline Command Line Options

The df_native example uses the following command line options to control spectral rendering at runtime:

- \c --spectral
  Enables spectral rendering support. When this flag is set, the renderer switches to spectral
  data types and APIs instead of the RGB variants.

- \c --spectral_min_wavelength (default: \c 400 nm)
  Sets the lower bound of the sampled wavelength interval.

- \c --spectral_max_wavelength (default: \c 700 nm)
  Sets the upper bound of the sampled wavelength interval.

The \c --spectral_min_wavelength and \c --spectral_max_wavelength parameters define the
wavelength range used by the example's wavelength sampling.

The pre-existing \c --cr option, which enables the MDL Custom Texture Runtime, is also relevant
when running with \c --spectral: it allows the application to provide its own implementations of
the RGB to spectral conversion callbacks instead of relying on the SDK's built-in conversions
(see \ref mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_conversion).

\subsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_backend Enabling Spectral Code Generation in the Backend

To enable spectral evaluation in the code generated by the MDL backend, the example sets
a backend option on the #mi::neuraylib::IMdl_backend instance:

\code
// enable spectral rendering
if (enable_spectral)
    check_success(be_native->set_option("libbsdf_enable_spectral", "on") == 0);
\endcode

Setting \c libbsdf_enable_spectral to \c "on" activates the spectral variants of the BSDF/EDF
code paths in libbsdf.
This will also change the return type for all compiled MDL expressions of type \c color from \c float3 to \c tct_spectral_sample, like the material IOR, emission intensity and volume coefficients.

The option is supported by the \c MB_NATIVE, \c MB_HLSL, and \c MB_GLSL backends and is set in
exactly the same way on each of them; the behavior is identical regardless of the target
language.

\subsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_shader_defines Propagating Spectral Configuration to Shader Compilers

When the MDL backend produces shader source (HLSL for DXR, GLSL for Vulkan), the generated code
references the preprocessor macros \c MDL_SPECTRAL_RENDERING and \c MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES. Unlike
the native backend, where \c MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES is baked in via
<tt>\<mi/neuraylib/target_code_types.h\></tt> at C++ compile time, shaders are compiled separately
by your renderer's shader toolchain and therefore receive these values from the host application.

There are two ways to do this in the examples:

- Passing the macros as defines to the shader compiler. The df_vulkan example uses this approach for
  its renderer shaders:

  \code
  // Vulkan
  defines.push_back("MDL_SPECTRAL_RENDERING");
  defines.push_back("MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES=" MI_BASE_STRINGIZE(MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES));
  \endcode

  The dxr example also uses this approach when compiling its non-MDL HLSL shaders (ray
  generation, miss programs, etc.):

  \code
  // DXR (non-MDL HLSL shaders)
  defines["MDL_SPECTRAL_RENDERING"] = "1";
  defines["MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES"] = std::to_string(MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES);
  \endcode

- Prepending \c \#define directives directly to the shader source string before it is handed to
  the shader compiler. The dxr example uses this approach for the per-material HLSL source that
  contains the MDL-generated code, where the same macros are emitted as text at the top of the
  source:

  \code
  // DXR (per-material HLSL source)
  if (m_app->get_options()->enable_spectral)
  {
      m_hlsl_source_code += "#define MDL_SPECTRAL_RENDERING\n";
      m_hlsl_source_code += "#define MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES " +
          std::to_string(MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES) + "\n";
  }
  \endcode

In all cases, the value passed to the shader compiler must match the C++ value of
\c MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES (configurable via the CMake option \c MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES_OVERRIDE).

\subsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_state Spectral Shading State Types and Per-Path Wavelength Sampling

In order to pass the current wavelength sample from the renderer into MDL-generated code, the SDK
provides spectral variants of the material shading state:

\code
mi::neuraylib::Shading_state_material_spectral shading_state;
mi::neuraylib::Shading_state_material_spectral_with_derivs shading_state_derivs;
\endcode

The spectral variants add the following field:

\code
tct_spectral_sample spectral_wavelengths;
\endcode

The \c spectral_wavelengths field allows your renderer to pass the wavelength samples associated
with the current ray path through the MDL code to the texture runtime which also handles spectral conversions.
It is not directly accessed by the generated code.

For simplicity, the example uses the same spectral material state for both,
RGB and spectral rendering, but for the RGB case (or if spectral rendering is not required) one can also use the original material state
variants:

\code
mi::neuraylib::Shading_state_material shading_state;
mi::neuraylib::Shading_state_material_with_derivs shading_state_derivs;
\endcode

\par Per-Path Wavelength Sampling

Each primary ray needs a fresh set of \c MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES wavelengths, with the value at
index \c 0 acting as the main wavelength used by libbsdf for importance sampling. The df_native
example uses stratified sampling with a random shift across the configured wavelength range and stores
them in the shading state for the lifetime of the path:

\code
if (rc.enable_spectral)
{
    // sample wavelengths for this path (stratified sampling with a random shift)
    tct_spectral_sample lambdas;
    float xi = rnd(seed);
    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES; ++i)
    {
        xi += 1.0f / float(MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES);
        if (xi > 1.0f)
            xi -= 1.0f;
        lambdas.values[i] =
            rc.spectral.min_wavelength
                + (rc.spectral.max_wavelength - rc.spectral.min_wavelength) * xi;
    }
    rc.shading_state.spectral_wavelengths = lambdas;

    // initialize the per-wavelength PDF ratios used for MIS along the path
    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES - 1; ++i)
        rc.spectral_pdf_ratios[i] = 1.0f;
}
\endcode

The wavelengths are sampled once per path and remain unchanged for all subsequent bounces, which
is required for the MIS bookkeeping described in \ref mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_weight.
On the GPU backends the same sampling is performed in the ray generation shader; the dxr
\c ray_gen_program.hlsl and the df_vulkan \c path_trace.comp use the same stratified sampling
scheme.

\subsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_bsdf Spectral BSDF/EDF Data Structures

During rendering, the df_native path tracer calls the usual MDL target code entry points to
account for the different light path contributions:

- #mi::neuraylib::ITarget_code::execute()
- #mi::neuraylib::ITarget_code::execute_edf_evaluate()
- #mi::neuraylib::ITarget_code::execute_bsdf_auxiliary()
- #mi::neuraylib::ITarget_code::execute_bsdf_evaluate()
- #mi::neuraylib::ITarget_code::execute_bsdf_sample()

These functions are the same in RGB and spectral mode; only the data structures used to pass
inputs and read back outputs differ. For the BSDF/EDF functions, each of the corresponding
data structures is a class template parameterized by a color mode enum:

\code
// Spectral rendering enabled or disabled depending on the backend and its configuration.
enum Target_code_color_mode : tct_int
{
    TCCM_RGB = 0,               // no spectral sampling, use RGB
    TCCM_SPECTRAL_SAMPLING = 1, // spectral sampling, use spectral samples
};
\endcode

- \c TCCM_RGB selects the regular RGB layout: colors and IORs are \c tct_float3 and PDFs are
  scalar \c tct_float.
- \c TCCM_SPECTRAL_SAMPLING selects the spectral layout: the same fields are replaced by
  \c tct_spectral_sample so that one value per wavelength sample is carried.

All BSDF/EDF data structures are templated on \c Target_code_color_mode and have explicit 
specializations for RGB and spectral sampling.  
The following excerpt illustrates this for \c Bsdf_sample_data:

\code
// Explicit specialization for RGB case
template<>
struct Bsdf_sample_data<TCCM_RGB> : public Bsdf_sample_data_base
{
    tct_float3       ior1;           ///< mutual input: IOR current medium
    tct_float3       ior2;           ///< mutual input: IOR other side
    tct_float3       k1;             ///< mutual input: outgoing direction

    tct_float3       k2;             ///< output: incoming direction

    tct_float4_a16   xi;             ///< input: pseudo-random sample numbers in range [0, 1)

    tct_float        pdf;            ///< output: pdf (non-projected hemisphere)
    tct_float3       bsdf_over_pdf;  ///< output: bsdf * dot(normal, k2) / pdf
    Bsdf_event_type  event_type;     ///< output: the type of event for the generated sample
    tct_int          handle;         ///< output: handle of the sampled elemental BSDF (lobe)

    Df_flags         flags;          ///< input: flags controlling calculation of result
                                     ///<     (optional depending on backend options)
};

// Explicit specialization for spectral sampling case
template<>
struct Bsdf_sample_data<TCCM_SPECTRAL_SAMPLING> : public Bsdf_sample_data_base
{
    tct_spectral_sample ior1;           ///< mutual input: IOR current medium
    tct_spectral_sample ior2;           ///< mutual input: IOR other side
    tct_float3          k1;             ///< mutual input: outgoing direction

    tct_float3          k2;             ///< output: incoming direction

    tct_float4_a16      xi;             ///< input: pseudo-random sample numbers in range [0, 1)

    tct_spectral_sample pdf;            ///< output: pdf (non-projected hemisphere)
    tct_spectral_sample bsdf_over_pdf;  ///< output: bsdf * dot(normal, k2) / pdf
    Bsdf_event_type     event_type;     ///< output: the type of event for the generated sample
    tct_int             handle;         ///< output: handle of the sampled elemental BSDF (lobe)

    Df_flags            flags;          ///< input: flags controlling calculation of result
                                        ///<     (optional depending on backend options)
};
\endcode

The same pattern applies to \c Edf_evaluate_data, \c Bsdf_auxiliary_data, and 
\c Bsdf_evaluate_data: the RGB variants use \c tct_float3 for color-like fields, while the 
spectral variants replace those fields by \c tct_spectral_sample.

\subsubsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_bsdf_shader Macro-based variants for HLSL and GLSL

HLSL and GLSL do not have C++ templates, so the SDK shader headers provide the same
RGB-versus-spectral switch through preprocessor macros instead of \c Target_code_color_mode
specializations. The color and PDF field types in the BSDF/EDF data structures are typedefs
that resolve to either scalar/RGB types or the \c Spectral_sample struct depending on whether
\c MDL_SPECTRAL_RENDERING is defined:

\code
#ifdef MDL_SPECTRAL_RENDERING
struct Spectral_sample { float values[MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES]; };
#define Color_sample Spectral_sample
#define Pdf_sample   Spectral_sample
#else
#define Color_sample vec3   // float3 in HLSL
#define Pdf_sample   float
#endif
\endcode

Because shading languages also lack operator overloading on user-defined structs, the SDK
shader headers additionally provide small helper functions for per-wavelength arithmetic
on \c Color_sample values, namely \c addcc (component-wise add), \c mulcc (component-wise
multiply) and \c mulcf (component times scalar). The renderer uses these helpers in the
spectral code paths in place of the regular \c + and \c * operators that work on \c float3.

The net effect is the same as in the C++ case: a single set of call sites and BSDF/EDF data
structure declarations works for both modes, and the only thing that changes is the underlying
field type. To stay consistent across backends, refer to color-valued fields as \c Color_sample
and PDFs as \c Pdf_sample in your renderer's shader code, instead of hard-coding \c float3 or
\c float.

\subsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_weight Spectral Ray Weight

In the original df_native example (RGB rendering), the ray weight is a \c float3:

\code
ray.weight = 1.f; // of type mi::Float32_3
\endcode

For spectral rendering, the example adds a parallel field of type \c tct_spectral_sample that
holds one ray weight per wavelength:

\code
ray.weight_spectral = make_spectral_sample(1.0f);
\endcode

Here, \c make_spectral_sample is a convenience helper that initializes all entries of the
spectral sample with the same scalar value. During path tracing, \c weight_spectral is
multiplied with the spectral BSDF/EDF values at each interaction, in the same way the
\c float3 \c weight is used in the RGB version.

\par Spectral MIS (\c update_spectral_pdf_ratios)

libbsdf samples a direction at the main wavelength (index \c 0) but returns a per-wavelength
PDF, since any BSDF may have different PDFs at the secondary wavelengths. The straightforward
spectral weight \c bsdf \c / \c pdf_main can therefore have high variance at the secondary
wavelengths, so the example applies single-sample MIS weights based on the per-wavelength PDF
ratios, tracked in \c spectral_pdf_ratios in the render context (see
\ref mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_state for initialization). The
\c update_spectral_pdf_ratios helper is called at every BSDF sample to update those ratios and
returns the factor that converts the previous MIS weight to the updated one:

\code
// in the render context
float spectral_pdf_ratios[MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES - 1];

float update_spectral_pdf_ratios(
    const tct_spectral_sample pdfs,        // per-wavelength PDFs returned by the BSDF sample
    const bool specular,                   // event was a specular bounce
    const bool specular_dispersion)        // specular bounce on a dispersive interface
{
    // the main wavelength (index 0) was used for sampling, so the MIS weight is
    //   w = p[0] / sum(p) = 1 / (sum(p) / p[0])

    if (specular && !specular_dispersion)  // pure specular: nothing to update
        return 1.0f;
    if (!specular_dispersion && pdfs.values[0] <= 0.0f)
        return 0.0f;                        // path has zero probability at the main wavelength

    float inv_w_old = 1.0f;
    float inv_w_new = 1.0f;
    const float inv_p0 = specular_dispersion ? 0.0f : (1.0f / pdfs.values[0]);
    for (unsigned int i = 1; i < MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES; ++i)
    {
        inv_w_old += spectral_pdf_ratios[i - 1];
        spectral_pdf_ratios[i - 1] *= pdfs.values[i] * inv_p0;
        inv_w_new += spectral_pdf_ratios[i - 1];
    }
    return inv_w_old / inv_w_new;
}
\endcode

The returned factor scales the spectral weight in-place after multiplying in \c bsdf_over_pdf:

\code
ray.weight_spectral *= sample_data.bsdf_over_pdf;
ray.weight_spectral *= rc.update_spectral_pdf_ratios(
    sample_data.pdf, specular, specular_dispersion);
\endcode

The dxr and df_vulkan examples carry the same \c spectral_pdf_ratios array in their per-ray state
and apply the same update rule.

\subsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_conversion RGB to Spectral Conversion

\note Spectral support only operates at the BSDF/EDF level for now; all input data (colors,
textures, etc.) remain RGB.

The code generated for spectral rendering must therefore be able to convert RGB values to
spectral samples for the wavelengths that are currently sampled, so that RGB-valued material
parameters and texture lookups can be consumed by the spectral BSDF/EDF code. In addition, the
renderer typically needs the inverse direction in order to present results:

- RGB to spectral (upsampling), used by the generated code when MDL parameters or texture
  fetches deliver RGB values that have to be evaluated spectrally.
- Spectral to RGB (downsampling), used by the renderer when writing RGB frame buffers for
  display, when storing image files, or when interacting with RGB-only APIs.

For RGB to spectral upsampling, the MDL SDK provides a default built-in implementation based on
Jendersie, "Fast Spectral Upsampling of Volume Attenuation Coefficients", Ray Tracing Gems II,
which can be used directly when spectral rendering is enabled. If you require full control,
you can override or replace these conversions via the MDL Custom Texture Runtime interface.

In the df_native example, the MDL Custom Texture Runtime is enabled through the pre-existing
\c --cr command line option. The following texture runtime callbacks can then be implemented to
handle the different kinds of RGB inputs consistently (IOR, reflectance, luminance, and volume
coefficients):

\code
// Implementation of rgb_to_spectral_ior().
void rgb_to_spectral_ior(
    tct_spectral_sample         *result,
    Texture_handler_base const  *self_base,
    Shading_state_material      *state,
    float const                  rgb[3])
{
    *result = rgb_to_spectral_ior(get_wavelengths(state), rgb);
}

// Implementation of rgb_to_spectral_reflectance().
void rgb_to_spectral_reflectance(
    tct_spectral_sample         *result,
    Texture_handler_base const  *self_base,
    Shading_state_material      *state,
    float const                  rgb[3])
{
    *result = rgb_to_spectral<false>(get_wavelengths(state), rgb);
}

// Implementation of rgb_to_spectral_luminance().
void rgb_to_spectral_luminance(
    tct_spectral_sample         *result,
    Texture_handler_base const  *self_base,
    Shading_state_material      *state,
    float const                  rgb[3])
{
    *result = rgb_to_spectral<true>(get_wavelengths(state), rgb);
}

// Implementation of rgb_to_spectral_volume_coefficient().
void rgb_to_spectral_volume_coefficient(
    tct_spectral_sample         *result,
    Texture_handler_base const  *self_base,
    Shading_state_material      *state,
    float const                  rgb[3])
{
    *result = rgb_to_spectral<false>(get_wavelengths(state), rgb);
}

// Implementation of get_wavelengths().
void get_wavelengths(
    tct_spectral_sample         *result,
    Texture_handler_base const  *self_base,
    Shading_state_material      *state)
{
    *result = get_wavelengths(state);
}
\endcode

The shared header \c texture_support_native.h additionally provides \c _deriv variants of each
callback that take \c Shading_state_material_with_derivs instead of \c Shading_state_material.
Your renderer only needs to register the variant that matches the mode for which target code was
generated; either the non-deriv set or the deriv set is required, never both.

For the actual implementations of the inline upsampling routines used here (\c rgb_to_spectral
and \c rgb_to_spectral_ior), please take a look at the source code section
\ref example_spectral_rendering, \c examples/mdl_sdk/shared/texture_support_native.h.

On the GPU backends (HLSL for DXR, GLSL for Vulkan) the same set of conversions has to be
provided, but the binding mechanism is different. There is no \c Texture_handler to register;
instead, the renderer's runtime shader file declares the conversion functions directly, with the
names that MDL-generated code expects, and they are linked at shader compile time. The expected
symbols are the same set documented above, expressed as shader functions:

\code
// HLSL (dxr/content/mdl_renderer_runtime.hlsl)
Spectral_sample mdl_rgb_to_spectral_ior(inout Shading_state_material state, float3 rgb);
Spectral_sample mdl_rgb_to_spectral_reflectance(inout Shading_state_material state, float3 rgb);
Spectral_sample mdl_rgb_to_spectral_luminance(inout Shading_state_material state, float3 rgb);
Spectral_sample mdl_rgb_to_spectral_volume_coefficient(inout Shading_state_material state, float3 rgb);
Spectral_sample mdl_get_wavelengths(inout Shading_state_material state);
\endcode

\code
// GLSL (df_vulkan/mdl_runtime.glsl)
Spectral_sample mdl_rgb_to_spectral_ior(inout State state, vec3 rgb);
Spectral_sample mdl_rgb_to_spectral_reflectance(inout State state, vec3 rgb);
Spectral_sample mdl_rgb_to_spectral_luminance(inout State state, vec3 rgb);
Spectral_sample mdl_rgb_to_spectral_volume_coefficient(inout State state, vec3 rgb);
Spectral_sample mdl_get_wavelengths(inout State state);
\endcode

When writing to an RGB frame buffer, the renderer needs to transform the spectral samples back
to the RGB color space. The exact implementation is renderer-specific and not dictated by the MDL SDK. 
The df_native example uses the following implementation:

\code
    // spectral sample to RGB conversion
    mi::Float32_3 spectral_to_rgb(const tct_spectral_sample &intensities, const bool is_reflectivity = false) const
    {
        mi::Float32_3 xyz(0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f);
        // weight by CIE XYZ color matching functions
        // (using Wyman et al - "Simple Analytic Approximations to the CIE XYZ Color Matching Functions")
        const tct_spectral_sample lambdas = get_wavelengths();
        for (unsigned int i = 0; i < MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES; ++i)
        {
            const float lambda = lambdas.values[i];
            // for reflectivity values we need to multiply by the spectral whitepoint of the RGB color space (normalized to luminance 1)
            const float factor = is_reflectivity ? ::lookup_d65(lambda) : 1.0f;
            {
                const float p1 = (lambda - 442.0f) * ((lambda < 442.0f) ? 0.0624f : 0.0374f);
                const float p2 = (lambda - 599.8f) * ((lambda < 599.8f) ? 0.0264f : 0.0323f);
                const float p3 = (lambda - 501.1f) * ((lambda < 501.1f) ? 0.0490f : 0.0382f);
                xyz.x +=
                    (0.362f * expf(-0.5f * p1 * p1) + 1.056f * expf(-0.5f * p2 * p2) - 0.065f * expf(-0.5f * p3 * p3)) * intensities.values[i] * factor;
            }
            {
                const float p1 = (lambda - 568.8f) * ((lambda < 568.8f) ? 0.0213f : 0.0247f);
                const float p2 = (lambda - 530.9f) * ((lambda < 530.9f) ? 0.0613f : 0.0322f);
                xyz.y +=
                    (0.821f * expf(-0.5f * p1 * p1) + 0.286f * expf(-0.5f * p2 * p2)) * intensities.values[i] * factor;
            }
            {
                const float p1 = (lambda - 437.0f) * ((lambda < 437.0f) ? 0.0845f : 0.0278f);
                const float p2 = (lambda - 459.0f) * ((lambda < 459.0f) ? 0.0385f : 0.0725f);
                xyz.z +=
                    (1.217f * expf(-0.5f * p1 * p1) + 0.681f * expf(-0.5f * p2 * p2)) * intensities.values[i] * factor;
            }
        }

        // apply scaling from radiometric to photometric units
        xyz *= 683.002f;

        // MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES samples uniformly on wavelength range
        xyz *= (spectral.max_wavelength - spectral.min_wavelength) / (float) MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES;

        // convert to linear sRGB
        return mi::Float32_3(
            dot(xyz, mi::Float32_3( 3.240600f, -1.537200f, -0.498600f)),
            dot(xyz, mi::Float32_3(-0.968900f,  1.875800f,  0.041500f)),
            dot(xyz, mi::Float32_3( 0.055700f, -0.204000f,  1.057000f)));
    }

\endcode

\subsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_backend_notes Backend-Specific Notes

The integration steps described above are common to all three reference examples. The following
notes capture aspects that are specific to a particular backend and therefore do not show up
in the df_native source but are required for a correct integration in DXR or Vulkan.

\subsubsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_dxr_notes DXR

In a recursive DXR pipeline, the path state does not live on a single C++ stack frame as in the
native example: it is carried across closest-hit invocations through the ray payload. For
spectral rendering the payload is adapted to include all per-path data that BSDF/EDF
evaluations need to consume:

- the per-path spectral weights (\c MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES floats instead of \c float3 now)
- the active wavelengths for the current path (\c MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES floats), so each
  closest-hit shader can re-establish \c state.spectral_wavelengths
- the per-wavelength PDF ratios used by \c update_spectral_pdf_ratios
  (\c MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES \c - \c 1 floats), so MIS is consistent across bounces.

The active wavelengths must be copied from the payload into the MDL shading state at the
beginning of every closest-hit shader, before any MDL-generated function is called:

\code
#if defined(MDL_SPECTRAL_RENDERING)
    // copy the active wavelengths from the ray payload into the shading state
    mdl_state.spectral_wavelengths = payload.spectral_wavelengths;
#endif
\endcode

Because the payload is larger in spectral mode, the DXR pipeline state object must declare the
correct maximum payload size up front. The dxr example computes it dynamically based on the
spectral mode:

\code
size_t payload_size = 10 * sizeof(float) + 2 * sizeof(uint32_t);
if (options->enable_spectral) {
    // spectral weight and wavelengths, and the spectral PDF ratios
    payload_size += (2 * MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES + (MDL_DF_SPECTRAL_SAMPLES - 1)) * sizeof(float);
} else {
    // float3 weight
    payload_size += 3 * sizeof(float);
}
pipeline->set_max_payload_size(payload_size);
\endcode

\subsubsection mi_neuray_example_spectral_rendering_vulkan_notes Vulkan

The df_vulkan example does not use the Vulkan ray tracing extension; it is a
compute-shader-based path tracer. As a consequence it does not need a ray payload, and there is
no equivalent of the DXR payload-size step. The spectral path state instead lives in a
per-thread \c Ray_state struct that contains the same fields as the DXR payload (spectral
weights, active wavelengths, per-wavelength PDF ratios). As in the DXR case, the wavelengths
must still be copied into \c state.spectral_wavelengths before each MDL-generated BSDF/EDF
function is invoked:

\code
#if defined(MDL_SPECTRAL_RENDERING)
    mdl_state.spectral_wavelengths = ray_state.spectral_wavelengths;
#endif
\endcode

In all GPU cases, the key invariant is that \c state.spectral_wavelengths must reflect the
wavelengths of the current path before any MDL-generated BSDF/EDF function is called.

